0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.7",{"topic":{"id":"4.7","title":"Memory and Log Analysis","minutes":15,"summary":"Combine volatile state with persistent records to reconstruct events and test whether a suspicious process or logon was actually active.","sections":[{"title":"Memory acquisition and interpretation","body":["Memory can reveal running processes, loaded modules, network connections, injected regions, and other state absent from disk. Acquisition requires a suitable tool, privileges, and a documented risk decision. The tool changes the machine while collecting evidence. Preserve the output, record the profile or symbol requirements, and explain incomplete or failed analysis.","A memory-analysis framework such as Volatility can enumerate artifacts using operating-system structures. Version and symbol mismatches can produce misleading results. A process name is not a validated identity; examine path, parent-child relationships, command line, loaded components, and corroborating telemetry. Sensitive content in memory must be access-controlled."]},{"title":"Log semantics and event reconstruction","body":["Windows Security, System, Application, and Sysmon logs answer different questions. Event IDs must be interpreted with provider, version, and configuration in mind. A logon event does not automatically prove interactive human presence; network, service, and remote logon types differ. Linux authentication, service, audit, and application logs similarly require context.","Build a timeline using event time, ingestion time, and acquisition time separately. Check clock drift, time zones, log forwarding delays, and missing sequences. A cleared log may be relevant, but rotation and administrative maintenance can also remove records. Preserve the original log files and document any parsing or conversion."]},{"title":"Correlation and uncertainty","body":["Correlate memory observations with disk artifacts and network records. A process seen in RAM may explain a connection that disk logs alone cannot. Conversely, a persistent service configuration may explain a process that initially looked unusual. Seek independent support before concluding malicious execution or deliberate log tampering.","Report the capture time and the limits of historical inference. A memory image is a snapshot, not a complete recording of everything that happened. A log timeline is limited by what was configured, retained, and successfully collected. Clear separation between observation and interpretation keeps the reconstruction credible."]}],"practice":{"title":"Explain a logon event","scenario":"A supplied log records a network logon followed by a service process and an outbound connection.","steps":["Identify the logon type and account context.","Compare the process and connection times with clock uncertainty.","Check whether scheduled maintenance offers a plausible baseline explanation.","Write a finding that distinguishes account activity from human attribution."],"debrief":"Event correlation can establish a sequence without establishing intent. The log’s semantics determine how far the conclusion can go."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","mitre"],"labId":"network","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain memory acquisition and interpretation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain log semantics and event reconstruction and apply it to an investigation.","Explain correlation and uncertainty and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.7","prompt":"Why is a memory image insufficient to reconstruct the entire incident history?","options":["It captures state at a particular time and may omit earlier activity.","It contains only photographs.","It cannot be hashed.","It always proves every process is malicious."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Memory is a time-specific observation; persistent logs and other evidence are needed for a broader history.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
